    <!-- LESSON ACCORDIONS SECTION -->
    <section class="section" id="lessons">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Core Signal Architecture</h2>
        <p>Master the five tactical lessons for turning experience into high-signal artifacts.</p>
      </div>

      <details class="lesson">
        <summary>Lesson 6 — Signal design: making evidence scannable</summary>
        <div class="lesson-body">
          <h4>The central idea</h4>
          <p>Evaluators spend under 45 seconds scanning your portfolio before deciding whether to read further. Signal design is the discipline of making the most important evidence visible in that first glance.</p>
          <h4>Signal design principles</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Lead with the metric:</strong> Open with the measurable outcome, not the setup.</li>
            <li><strong>Use visual hierarchy:</strong> Large numbers, bold labels, and whitespace direct the eye.</li>
            <li><strong>Front-load the decision:</strong> State the tradeoff you made before describing the artifact.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove decoration:</strong> Every visual element should answer an evaluator question.</li>
          </ul>
          <h4>Practice</h4>
          <p>Take your strongest project and reduce it to three lines: one metric, one decision, one verification link. If the three lines do not create curiosity, the signal is not yet strong enough.</p>
          <div class="callout"><strong>Expert insight:</strong> A portfolio that is easy to scan is more likely to be read deeply.</div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="lesson">
        <summary>Lesson 7 — Provenance and verification: making claims inspectable</summary>
        <div class="lesson-body">
          <h4>The central idea</h4>
          <p>A claim that cannot be inspected is not evidence — it is a request for trust. Verification turns confidence into an inspectable artifact.</p>
          <h4>Verification ladder</h4>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Stated claim:</strong> "I improved the process."</li>
            <li><strong>Documented claim:</strong> "I improved the process, here is the log."</li>
            <li><strong>Measured claim:</strong> "I reduced cycle time from 12 days to 4 days."</li>
            <li><strong>Reproducible claim:</strong> "Here is the dataset and method so you can verify the measurement."</li>
            <li><strong>Independently validated claim:</strong> "An external auditor confirmed the reduction."</li>
          </ol>
          <h4>Practice</h4>
          <p>For each major claim, identify which rung of the ladder you are currently on and what would be required to climb one rung.</p>
          <div class="callout"><strong>Design rule:</strong> Attach at least one inspectable link, file, or artifact to every case study.</div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="lesson">
        <summary>Lesson 8 — Portfolio as production system: building career capital</summary>
        <div class="lesson-body">
          <h4>The central idea</h4>
          <p>A portfolio is not a one-time project. It is a production system that continuously converts research into credibility, and credibility into opportunity.</p>
          <h4>The production loop</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Research:</strong> Investigate a real problem.</li>
            <li><strong>Insight:</strong> Extract a reusable principle.</li>
            <li><strong>Framework:</strong> Name and structure the principle so it can be taught.</li>
            <li><strong>Artifact:</strong> Build something that demonstrates the framework.</li>
            <li><strong>Publication:</strong> Put the artifact where it can be inspected.</li>
            <li><strong>Feedback:</strong> Collect reactions and iterate.</li>
          </ul>
          <h4>Example</h4>
          <p>One article can become a conference presentation, a LinkedIn post, an interview example, a workshop, a newsletter, a podcast discussion, a training module, a portfolio page, a client proposal, and a book chapter.</p>
          <div class="callout"><strong>Expert insight:</strong> Stop treating the portfolio as a showcase. Treat it as a factory that compounds credibility.</div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="lesson">
        <summary>Lesson 9 — The visibility flywheel: from research to authority</summary>
        <div class="lesson-body">
          <h4>The central idea</h4>
          <p>Authority is not granted — it is accumulated through a repeatable flywheel. Each stage of the flywheel feeds the next, creating compounding career capital.</p>
          <h4>The flywheel stages</h4>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Research:</strong> Investigate a meaningful problem.</li>
            <li><strong>Insight:</strong> Extract a principle worth sharing.</li>
            <li><strong>Framework:</strong> Structure the insight into a teachable model.</li>
            <li><strong>Artifact:</strong> Build something that demonstrates it.</li>
            <li><strong>Publication:</strong> Publish where the right people can find it.</li>
            <li><strong>Feedback:</strong> Collect responses and iterate.</li>
            <li><strong>Improvement:</strong> Strengthen the artifact based on feedback.</li>
